{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Design addendum — two feasibility questions surfaced by plan-review, resolved\n\n**(G6) The write-fence needs NO clone registry.** The original escalations correctly note the tracker has no registry of clones, so a *client-side* global quiesce is impossible. It is not needed: every clone auto-pushes to the ONE `sync.remote`, which is the single serialization choke point. The fence is a SERVER-SIDE ref-lock / pre-receive hold on the remote's `tickets` ref for the brief swap window (precedent: ADR 0031 reconciler-ref-lock, ADR 0011 mirror-lock). Any event that reached the remote before the fence is captured in the re-derived baseline; pushes arriving during the window are rejected and retried against the new tip — so no ACKNOWLEDGED write is ever lost. A <N-day clone's UNPUSHED events reconcile by EVENT-FILE REPLAY onto the new baseline (reset the tracking ref to the baseline, re-stage the append-only local-only event files, commit) rather than a 3-way history merge, so the absent common ancestor after the rewrite is irrelevant. Only a >N-day offline clone loses divergent work — the explicitly accepted loss.\n\n**(T3) The history-rewrite primitive is core-git, not third-party.** The reclamation operation emits the new history with `git fast-import` (equivalently `git mktree` + `git commit-tree` plumbing), all of which ship with git — so the disposable clone requires no `git filter-repo` (which is not vendored in this repo). Because each ticket's as-of-horizon state already exists as a committed SNAPSHOT, the baseline is synthesized by combining every ticket's SNAPSHOT tree into one root commit, then replaying the in-horizon commits on top; the operation's preflight asserts `git fast-import --version`. This is what backs the portability claim (no single-CI-provider or third-party-tool dependence).\n\nThese are folded into the ticket description (Feasibility section) and the S0 ADR must carry the formal proof that the remote-side fence + event-file-replay reconciliation loses no acknowledged/observable work for any clone state."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788037859304441917,"uuid":"2c216e6a-25a5-4b44-93c6-30de56a2f99a"}